format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-meets-matter

lesson ionizing-or-not
title: Ionizing or not
minutes: 5
xp: 50
teaches: u3-ionizing-meaning
teaches: u3-sort-radiation
relies_on: u2-recognise-radiation

description:
    Radiation can knock electrons out of atoms, or it can fall short. You will find out what ionizing means, and which common kinds of radiation are on which side.

cite ref-epa-radiation-basics

supports:
    Ionizing radiation has enough energy to remove electrons from atoms; non-ionizing radiation does not. Radio waves, visible light and microwaves are non-ionizing; alpha, beta, gamma and X-rays are ionizing.

cite ref-openstax-chem-21-6

supports:
    Ionizing radiation is energetic enough to knock electrons out of molecules: high-energy ultraviolet, X-rays, gamma rays and particles such as alpha and beta. Lower-frequency, lower-energy electromagnetic radiation is nonionizing.

cite ref-who-ionizing-radiation
supports: Ionizing radiation is radiation with enough energy to remove electrons from atoms, a process called ionization.

card c01
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Radiation is energy on the move
figure: radiation-is-energy
figure_at: 1

body:
    Radiation is energy that travels. It can travel as waves, or as tiny particles. Sunlight is radiation, and so are radio waves and gamma rays.

card c02
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Energy can hit an atom
figure: electron-knocked-out
figure_at: 2

body:
    Think of a ball rolling into a marble. A fast ball can knock the marble away. Some radiation hits an atom a bit like that, and knocks an electron right out of it.

card c03
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: That is ionization

body:
    Knocking an electron out of an atom is called ionization. Radiation with enough energy to do it is called [[ionizing-radiation|ionizing radiation]]. Weaker radiation can make atoms move or vibrate, but it cannot take an electron away.

card c04
kind: item
type: item
item: u3-l1-choice-ionizing
connected: true

card c05
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Four ionizing kinds
figure: energy-line
figure_at: 1

body:
    Alpha particles, beta particles, gamma rays and [[x-ray|X-rays]] are all ionizing. Alpha and beta are particles. Gamma rays and X-rays are waves of energy.

card c06
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Three that are not

body:
    Visible light, microwaves and radio waves are not ionizing. They do not have enough energy to remove electrons from atoms. They are still radiation, so the word radiation alone does not tell you which kind you have.

card c07
kind: item
type: item
item: u3-l1-tom-light
connected: true

